 { Candidate Moves Pogos Nakhapetiane – Sergei Zhigalko, Olginka 2011 Black to play Black had just given up the exchange to regain his earlier sacrificed pawn. Seemingly he was so glad about this that he failed to actually look at the position and the opportunities he has in it. Alexander Kotov famously introduced the idea of candidate moves into chess literature in Think Like a Grandmaster. The idea is simple and powerful. Rather than calculating endlessly on his first genius inspiration, Kotov suggested that the strong chess player would instead apply a bit of structure to his thinking and look for options first. Kotov then went from this sound position to a land of trees and mechanical thinking that no one has returned to with their sanity intact. We shall not repeat this mistake. Instead, we shall focus on candidate moves and ideas as a simple technique to become more creative. The idea is simple (as it should be) . By focusing on looking for ideas that have not revealed themselves at once to our divine selves, we open up for our human ability to discover something new – in this case about the position right in front of us. In my previous book on calculation, Excelling at Chess Calculation, I called the first chapter Before You Can Think, You Need To Learn How To See . This is what candidates are all about. } 1... -- 
 *

[Event "Grandmaster Preparation: Calculation"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/1525219/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "2.2"]
[White "2. Candidate Moves"]
[Black "David Berczes vs. Hans Tikkanen"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "r3r3/5Rpp/p1k5/P1p5/7N/6P1/2p2P1P/2R3K1 b - - 0 29"]

 { Let’s start with a simple example. David Berczes – Hans Tikkanen, Stockholm 2010 Black is a piece down and lost a long ending after } ( 28... Re2 $2 29. Nf3 Rae8 30. Kf1 { without real counterplay. If Tikkanen had used this moment to look at the position instead of thinking, chances are that he would have seen a much better move. } ) 
Re1+ $1 { Quite a surprising move if you have not spent a few moments looking at it. But once you see it, the idea is obvious. White cannot prevent Black from making something of his only asset and promoting the pawn. } 29. Rxe1 Rd8 $1 { A bit of accuracy. Obviously this decision can be classified as comparison, but in the end, all calculation techniques are based on looking for moves we have not seen immediately. The point is of course that } ( 29... Rb8 $2 { would land the rook on the wrong side of the pawn and allow White to eliminate it with } 30. Rfe7 $1 Rb1 31. R7e6 Kc7 32. R6e2 { when the extra piece will guarantee him an extra point. } ) 
30. Kg2 Rd1 31. Re6+ $1 { Black is now faced with a trickier decision, but no doubt it would be one Tikkanen would have been grateful to be allowed to make. } Rd6 { Black would be swimming in shark-infested waters after } ( 31... Kd5 $6 32. Rb6 c1=Q $11 33. Nf5 $1 Rg1 34. Kh3 Qf1 35. Kh4 Qc4 36. Kg5 { with great dangers for the black king. } ) 
32. Re2 c1=Q 33. Nf3 $14 { White has the advantage, but the game would have been open to three results. } 
 *

